# PHPMailer AddAddress() and Recipient Parsing

When working with `PHPMailer`, a common stumbling block involves misunderstanding how the library interprets recipient data versus how raw email headers format them. The source scenario illustrates a frequent misconception: passing a comma-separated string directly into `AddAddress()` expecting it to parse multiple addresses like an SMTP envelope.

The code snippet provided attempts to inject a single string containing multiple addresses separated by commas:

```php
$to = "me@example.com,you@example.net,she@example.it";
$obj->AddAddress($to);
```

In this context, `PHPMailer` treats the argument as a single recipient object. It does not automatically split the string on commas to create multiple distinct recipients within the message header. Instead, it sets the `To:` header field value exactly as provided, resulting in invalid syntax for the email client or causing the mail server to reject the message because the address list is malformed.

To successfully send to multiple individual recipients, you must instantiate separate `Address` objects (or use the method variants that accept arrays) for each unique recipient. The library expects distinct calls to define the structure of the recipient list properly.

```php
$recipient1 = "me@example.com";
$recipient2 = "you@example.net";
$recipient3 = "she@example.it";

$obj->AddAddress($recipient1);
$obj->AddAddress($recipient2);
$obj->AddAddress($recipient3);
```

Alternatively, if you are using the `addAddress()` method with the full signature including name, or utilizing the bulk-add variant `addAddress(array $addresses)`, you can pass an array of address strings. This is often cleaner when iterating over a list:

```php
$recipients = [
    "me@example.com",
    "you@example.net",
    "she@example.it"
];

foreach ($recipients as $recipient) {
    $obj->AddAddress($recipient);
}
// Or using the array signature if your version supports it:
// $obj->addAddress($recipients); 
```

### The Difference Between Recipient Types

A critical technical detail often overlooked is the distinction between who receives the email versus who is copied or blind-copied. If you pass a comma-separated string to `AddAddress()` without realizing the method signature, you might be confusing it with `AddCC()` or `AddBcc()`. However, even within those methods, passing a raw string of multiple addresses will not parse them into separate headers; they remain a single string value.

If your goal is to send one email where everyone sees each other's addresses, use `AddAddress()` for the primary recipients and `AddCC()` for carbon copies. If you want privacy where recipients don't see the others' emails in the header, you must use `AddBcc()`. Note that `AddBcc()` also requires distinct calls or an array; it will not parse a comma-separated string.

```php
// Primary recipients (visible to all)
$obj->AddAddress('user1@domain.com', 'First User');
$obj->AddAddress('user2@domain.com', 'Second User');

// Carbon copies (visible headers)
$obj->AddCC('cc_user@domain.com', 'CC User');

// Blind carbon copies (hidden from others)
$obj->AddBcc('secret@domain.com', 'Secret Recipient');
```

### Handling Names and Validation

When calling `AddAddress()`, you can optionally pass a second argument for the recipient's name. This is vital for professional communication, as email clients display "Name <email@example.com>" rather than just the address. If your data source provides names alongside emails, populate this field to improve deliverability and user experience.

Furthermore, always ensure that every email address follows RFC 5322 standards before passing it to PHPMailer. While PHPMailer has some built-in validation depending on its configuration, relying solely on the library to fix malformed input (like missing `@` symbols or spaces in the wrong places) is a fragile pattern. Pre-validating your recipient list ensures that `AddAddress()` receives clean data, preventing silent failures where the email bounces without clear error reporting.

### Conclusion

The `AddAddress()` method is not a parser for delimited strings; it is an object builder for individual recipients. Passing a comma-separated list as a single string violates the expected input structure and results in a malformed `To:` header. By issuing separate calls or passing arrays of distinct addresses, you ensure that PHPMailer constructs valid email headers that any standard mail transfer agent can process correctly.
